🔥 How to Tell Which Classifier is More Accurate (WEKA)
When you run classifiers in WEKA → Classify tab, look at this section:
________________________________________
✅ 1. Correctly Classified Instances (MOST IMPORTANT)
Example:
Correctly Classified Instances    13     92.85 %
Incorrectly Classified Instances   1      7.14 %
👉 What to say:
👉 “The classifier with higher correctly classified percentage is more accurate.”
✔ This is the primary metric
________________________________________
✅ 2. Accuracy Formula (if teacher asks)
👉 Accuracy =
"Correct Predictions" /"Total Instances" ×100

________________________________________
✅ 3. Confusion Matrix (VERY IMPORTANT)
Example:
   a   b   <-- classified as
   5   1   a = yes
   0   8   b = no
👉 How to explain:
	Diagonal values = correct predictions ✔ 
	Non-diagonal = errors ❌ 
👉 “Higher diagonal values indicate better classifier performance.”
________________________________________
✅ 4. Precision, Recall, F-Measure
You’ll see:
Metric	Meaning
Precision	How correct predictions are
Recall	How many actual cases are found
F-Measure	Balance of precision & recall
👉 What to say in viva:
👉 “Higher precision, recall and F-measure indicate better classifier performance.”
________________________________________
✅ 5. ROC Area (AUC) - Receiver Operating Characteristic
Example:
ROC Area = 0.95
👉 Range: 0 to 1
	Closer to 1 → better 
	0.5 → random 
👉 “Classifier with higher ROC value is more reliable.”
________________________________________
🔥 FINAL DECISION (What to Compare)
When comparing classifiers (e.g., J48 vs Naive Bayes):
👉 Say this:
	Check Accuracy % 
	Check F-Measure 
	Check ROC Area 
✔ The one with highest overall values = best classifier
________________________________________
